    "title": "Concept: Direct Proportion (y = kx)",
    "explanation": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">Direct proportion describes a relationship where two quantities increase or decrease at the same rate. This means if one quantity doubles, the other also doubles. When graphed, the relationship produces a straight line that passes through the origin (0,0). The constant <em>k</em> is called the constant of proportionality, and it shows the rate of change. For example, if <em>y = 3x</em>, then <em>k = 3</em> means y is always three times x.</p>",
